{"kind":"artifact","text":"docs/reference/architecture.md at merged main a694990675a4a2316d342037542553532361f127, restoring succession at this exact path after the merge published its successor at docs instead.\n\nWhy this exists. The merge of the CreateConfig repair retired my candidate 82a13ac185 at docs/reference/architecture.md and published its documentation successor at the path docs, which is the path that merge convention has succeeded 258 times. Paths match as exact strings, so a successor at docs flares nothing anchored to docs/reference/architecture.md, and that path was left holding one live artifact, my own stale b108f2b10b at d465b450. This artifact puts a live, current, unstale artifact back at the specific path so nothing anchored there dangles.\n\nWhat it describes is the merged state, not a candidate: the custody contract paragraph as it stands on main at a6949906. Creation writes and closes a privately named staging file in the metadata directory and hard-links the completed file to the destination, so exclusive creation holds and a concurrent reader sees the destination as absent or complete; a crash keeps no such promise, because nothing syncs the staging file or its directory before the link. Replacement is a separate, unchanged rename path, which is how initialization and every later save store the file, so the two paths do not accept the same filesystems and an attach can be refused where an initialization succeeds. The no-hard-link refusal is read from os.Link's single unbranched call site, not measured on such a filesystem.\n\nI am publishing this rather than leaving the path stale, and separately recording that two conventions are in use for this file so somebody decides which one wins instead of both persisting.","body":{"commit":"a694990675a4a2316d342037542553532361f127","path":"docs/reference/architecture.md"}}